    "durationDays": 5,
    "estimatedBudgetPerPersonInr": 14350,
    "travelStyle": "relaxed",
    "bestSeason": "Nov–Feb — dry, 20–32°C; the monsoon (Jun–Sep) closes beach shacks and turns the rivers brown",
    "travelTips": [
      "Book the self-drive car for the whole five days, not per-day: airport pickup and drop-off is where the money is saved.",
      "Do Old Goa early. The Basilica opens at 9:00 and by 11:00 the tour buses own the courtyard.",
      "North Goa is small enough to walk and short-hop; resist the urge to drive to every beach on the list.",
      "South Goa's beaches are 30–50 km from the north — moving base once, on day 4, is the right call.",
      "Carry cash for beach shacks, the fort gates and market stalls; cards work in hotels and big restaurants only."
    ],
    "warningsAndAssumptions": [
      "Entry fees: Old Goa's basilicas and the forts at Chapora and Cabo de Rama are free to enter. Fort Aguada charges a small ASI ticket that was not confirmed at publication — ₹100/person is budgeted as an allowance for it and other gate tickets.",
      "Palolem, Colva, Baga, Anjuna and Sinquerim beaches are free; sunbeds and water sports are paid locally and are not in this budget.",
      "Rental cars are priced at ₹5,500/person for five days (a compact self-drive hatchback shared by two, fuel excluded). The engine prices fuel separately at ₹96/L and 16 km/L — Goa's pump price sits in the state band, not the national one.",
      "The Wednesday Anjuna flea market is genuinely Wednesday-only; this plan does not depend on it.",
      "November is peak season: book the Candolim and Colva nights a month ahead, and expect shack prices to be the year's highest.",
      "All costs are engine estimates over real coordinates and cited fees, not quotes. Treat ±15% as the honest band."
